.TH SV_MAIN_UNIFIED 1 "January 2026" "Version 1.0" "SystemVerilog Decompiler"
.SH NAME
sv_main_unified \- SystemVerilog decompiler with multiple backend support
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sv_main_unified scan
.I backend output_dir
.RB [ --verify ]
.br
.B sv_main_unified file
.I backend json_file output_file
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sv_main_unified
is a SystemVerilog decompiler that converts Verilator's JSON AST representation back to synthesizable SystemVerilog or other target languages. It supports multiple backends for different output formats and use cases.

The tool processes Verilator-generated JSON AST files (*.tree.json) and produces readable, synthesizable output with optional formal verification.
.SH MODES
.TP
.B scan
Process all JSON files in the
.I obj_dir/
directory. This mode automatically discovers and processes all Verilator-generated AST files, excluding intermediate compiler passes and verification outputs.
.TP
.B file
Process a single JSON file. This mode allows fine-grained control over which file to process and where to write output.
.SH BACKENDS
.TP
.BR standard ", " std
Original SystemVerilog backend. Produces clean, readable SystemVerilog that closely matches the input structure. Best for code review and understanding Verilator's internal representation.

.I "Output format:"
SystemVerilog (.sv)
.br
.I "Use cases:"
Documentation, debugging, understanding compiled designs
.TP
.BR structural ", " struct
Structural backend with hardware primitives. Converts behavioral code to structural form using explicit primitives (DFFs, latches, muxes). Includes memory primitive inference for large arrays.

.I "Output format:"
SystemVerilog with primitives (.sv)
.br
.I "Requires:"
structural_primitives.sv
.br
.I "Use cases:"
FPGA synthesis, ASIC design, timing analysis
.br
.I "Features:"
Memory detection, register inference reporting (Synopsys DC style), conflict checking
.TP
.B yosys
Yosys-compatible backend. Produces output optimized for Yosys open synthesis suite. Uses Yosys-friendly constructs and avoids unsupported features.

.I "Output format:"
SystemVerilog (.sv)
.br
.I "Use cases:"
Open-source FPGA flows, formal verification with Yosys
.TP
.BR hardcaml ", " hc
HardCaml OCaml backend. Generates HardCaml hardware description code in OCaml. Enables functional hardware design with strong type safety and formal verification.

.I "Output format:"
SystemVerilog wrapper (.sv)
.br
.I "Use cases:"
Formal verification, functional hardware design, research
.br
.I "Features:"
Z3 equivalence checking, circuit construction API
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --verify
Enable Z3-based formal verification. After generation, the tool:
.RS
.IP 1. 3
Re-parses the generated output through Verilator
.IP 2.
Constructs Z3 SMT queries for both original and generated ASTs
.IP 3.
Proves functional equivalence via SMT solving
.IP 4.
Reports verification status (PASSED/FAILED)
.RE
.IP
.I "Note:"
Verification requires Z3 solver and adds significant processing time. Only available in
.B scan
mode.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success - all files processed without errors
.TP
.B 1
Error - invalid arguments, backend failure, or verification failure
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic Usage
Process all files with HardCaml backend:
.RS
.nf
.B sv_main_unified scan hardcaml results/
.fi
.RE

Process single file with structural backend:
.RS
.nf
.B sv_main_unified file structural input.tree.json output.sv
.fi
.RE

.SS Formal Verification
Process with verification enabled:
.RS
.nf
.B sv_main_unified scan structural results/ --verify
.fi
.RE
.IP
Output shows verification status:
.RS
.nf
Processing Vdesign.tree.json...
  [Verify] Parsing generated Verilog...
  [Verify] Running Z3 equivalence check...
  ✓ Verification PASSED: Functionally equivalent
.fi
.RE

.SS Backend Comparison
Process same design with all backends:
.RS
.nf
.B sv_main_unified scan standard results_std/
.B sv_main_unified scan structural results_struct/
.B sv_main_unified scan yosys results_yosys/
.B sv_main_unified scan hardcaml results_hc/
.fi
.RE

.SS Memory-Heavy Designs
For designs with large arrays, use structural backend:
.RS
.nf
.B sv_main_unified scan struct results/
.fi
.RE
.IP
The structural backend will:
.IP \(bu 3
Detect memory arrays (>128 bits)
.IP \(bu
Infer memory primitives (ROM, single-port, dual-port)
.IP \(bu
Check for memory access conflicts
.IP \(bu
Report register inference (Synopsys DC style)
.SH FILES
.TP
.I obj_dir/*.tree.json
Input files (Verilator JSON AST)
.TP
.I structural_primitives.sv
Hardware primitives library (required for structural backend)
.TP
.I results/warnings.txt
Warnings and diagnostics output
.TP
.I results/decompile_*.sv
Generated output files
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B VERILATOR_ROOT
Path to Verilator installation (optional)
.SH OUTPUT FORMAT
The tool generates different artifacts based on backend:
.SS Standard Backend
Clean SystemVerilog preserving original structure:
.RS
.nf
module example(input clk, output reg [7:0] q);
  always @(posedge clk) q <= q + 1;
endmodule
.fi
.RE

.SS Structural Backend
Explicit primitives with inference reports:
.RS
.nf
// Generated by structural converter
// Primitives defined in: structural_primitives.sv

module example(input clk, output [7:0] q);
  wire [7:0] q_next;
  dff_en #(.WIDTH(8)) q_dff(.clk(clk), .d(q_next),
                            .q(q), .en(1'b1));
  assign q_next = q + 8'd1;
endmodule
.fi
.RE
.IP
Followed by inference report:
.RS
.nf
Inferred memory devices in process
    in routine always_seq_1 line 0 in file 'example'.
===============================================
| Register Name    | Type | Width | Bus | AR |
===============================================
| q                | Flop |     8 |   Y | N  |
===============================================
| Total inferred registers:   1     Bits:   8 |
===============================================
.fi
.RE

.SS HardCaml Backend
OCaml HardCaml code:
.RS
.nf
(* Generated via HardCaml circuit construction *)
open Hardcaml
open Signal

let create ~clock ~clear (input : 'a I.t) =
  let spec = Reg_spec.create ~clock ~clear () in
  let q = reg spec (q +:. 1) in
  O.{ q }
.fi
.RE

.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.SS Warnings
The tool reports various warnings:
.TP
.B "Converting module 'X' to structural form"
Informational: structural transformation in progress
.TP
.B "Memory access conflict detected"
Error: Too many ports on memory array (max 2R/2W)
.TP
.B "Unsynthesizable always block"
Warning: Block contains non-synthesizable constructs
.TP
.B "Incomplete if statement"
Warning: Missing else branch in combinational logic

.SS Verification Messages
.TP
.B "✓ Verification PASSED"
Generated code is functionally equivalent to original
.TP
.B "✗ Verification FAILED"
Generated code differs from original (may indicate bug)
.TP
.B "✗ Verification EXCEPTION"
Verification process encountered error

.SH MEMORY DETECTION
The structural backend includes sophisticated memory detection:

.SS Memory Classification
.IP \(bu 3
.B "Small arrays"
(<128 bits): Expanded to individual registers
.IP \(bu
.B "Large arrays"
(≥128 bits): Converted to memory primitives
.IP \(bu
.B "Packed arrays"
Wide single-element arrays: Dimension inference

.SS Memory Primitives
.TP
.B memory_rom
Read-only memory (no write ports)
.TP
.B memory_sp
Single-port RAM (1 read, 1 write)
.TP
.B memory_1w2r
Dual-read, single-write RAM
.TP
.B memory_2w2r
True dual-port RAM (2 read, 2 write)
.TP
.B memory_2w2r_rf
Register file variant (optimized for high-frequency access)

.SS Conflict Detection
The tool checks for invalid memory access patterns:
.RS
.nf
Error:  Multiple conflicting accesses to memory 'mem'
        3 read port(s), 3 write port(s) detected
        Memory can support at most 2 read ports
        Consider: time-multiplexing or banking
.fi
.RE

.SH REGISTER INFERENCE
The structural backend reports all inferred registers in Synopsys Design Compiler style:

.SS Report Format
.RS
.nf
===============================================
| Register Name    | Type | Width | Bus | AR |
===============================================
| pipeline_reg1    | Flop |    32 |   Y | Y  |
| counter          | Flop |    16 |   Y | N  |
| flag             | Flop |     1 |   N | N  |
===============================================
| Total: 3 registers          Bits:     49   |
===============================================
.fi
.RE

.SS Column Meanings
.TP
.B Type
Always "Flop" (D flip-flop)
.TP
.B Width
Bit width (1 for scalar, N for vector)
.TP
.B Bus
Y if multi-bit register, N if single-bit
.TP
.B AR
Y if async reset present, N otherwise
.TP
.B AS
Y if async set present (reserved)
.TP
.B SR/SS
Sync reset/set (not implemented)

.SH WORKFLOW INTEGRATION
.SS Typical Usage Flow
.IP 1. 3
Design entry in SystemVerilog
.IP 2.
Verilator compilation:
.B verilator --json-only design.sv
.IP 3.
Decompilation:
.B sv_main_unified scan structural results/
.IP 4.
Review inference reports and warnings
.IP 5.
Use generated output for synthesis or verification

.SS Integration with Verilator
.RS
.nf
# Complete flow
verilator --json-only --top-module mycpu cpu.sv
sv_main_unified scan struct results/
# Review results/decompile_Vmycpu.tree.json.sv
.fi
.RE

.SS Integration with Synthesis Tools
Generated structural output can be synthesized:
.RS
.nf
# Synopsys Design Compiler
read_verilog structural_primitives.sv
read_verilog results/decompile_Vdesign.tree.json.sv
compile

# Yosys
yosys -p "read_verilog -sv results/decompile_*.sv; \\
          synth_xilinx -top design"
.fi
.RE

.SH PERFORMANCE
.SS Processing Speed
.TP
.B Standard backend
~1000 LOC/second (fast, minimal transformations)
.TP
.B Structural backend
~500 LOC/second (moderate, memory detection + inference)
.TP
.B HardCaml backend
~300 LOC/second (slower, circuit construction)
.TP
.B Verification
~50 LOC/second (very slow, Z3 SMT solving)

.SS Memory Usage
.IP \(bu 3
Typical: 50-200 MB per file
.IP \(bu
Large designs (>10K LOC): up to 1 GB
.IP \(bu
Verification: 2-3x base memory usage

.SH LIMITATIONS
.SS Unsupported Constructs
.IP \(bu 3
System tasks ($display, $finish) - stripped from output
.IP \(bu
Delays (#10) - not synthesizable
.IP \(bu
Initial blocks - removed (except parameter initialization)
.IP \(bu
Fork/join - not supported
.IP \(bu
DPI calls - not translated

.SS Backend-Specific Limitations
.TP
.B Structural
.IP \(bu 3
Requires all arrays <2R/2W ports
.IP \(bu
Cannot handle complex state machines (use standard backend)
.IP \(bu
Memory primitives must be provided separately
.TP
.B HardCaml
.IP \(bu 3
Sequential logic only (no latches)
.IP \(bu
Limited parametric polymorphism
.IP \(bu
Requires OCaml HardCaml library

.SH DEBUGGING
.SS Enable Verbose Output
Set environment variable:
.RS
.B SV_DEBUG=1 sv_main_unified scan struct results/
.RE

.SS Check Intermediate Files
.RS
.nf
.B ls obj_dir/*.tree.json
    # All input files
.B cat results/warnings.txt
    # All warnings
.B cat dump.json
    # Transformed AST (HardCaml backend)
.fi
.RE

.SS Common Issues
.TP
.B "No files processed"
Check that
.I obj_dir/
contains *.tree.json files
.TP
.B "Backend failed"
Try simpler backend (standard) first
.TP
.B "Verification failed"
Expected for non-equivalent optimizations; use
.B --verify
only for exact equivalence

.SH BUGS
.SS Known Issues
.IP \(bu 3
Some complex packed structs may not decompile correctly
.IP \(bu
Verification may timeout on large designs (>5K LOC)
.IP \(bu
Memory conflict detection is conservative (may report false positives)
.IP \(bu
HardCaml backend does not support tri-state logic

.SS Reporting Bugs
Report issues at: https://github.com/anthropics/sv-suite/issues

Include:
.IP \(bu 3
Input JSON file (or minimal reproducer)
.IP \(bu
Command line used
.IP \(bu
Full error output
.IP \(bu
Backend used

